==Old English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|ang|swefen}}

===Etymology===
{{root|ang|ine-pro|*swep-}}
From {{inh|ang|gmw-pro|*swefn}}, from {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*swefnaz||sleep, dream}}, from {{inh|ang|ine-pro|*swépnos}}. Cognate with {{cog|osx|sweƀan}}, {{cog|non|svefn}}. The [[w:Proto-Indo-European language|Indo-European]] root also led to {{cog|grc|ὕπνος}}, {{cog|la|somnus}}, {{cog|sga|suan}}, {{cog|cu|сънъ}}, {{cog|ru|сон}}, {{cog|lt|sãpnas}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ang-IPA|swefn}}

===Noun===
{{ang-noun|n}}

# [[dream]]
#: {{ux|ang|Iċ nǣfre ne ġeman mīn '''swefnu'''.|I never remember my '''dreams'''.}}

====Usage notes====
"To have a dream" was expressed with {{m|ang|mǣtan||to dream}}, not {{m|ang|habban||to have}}: ''Ġiestran niht mē <u>mǣtte</u> swefn þæt iċ wǣre fram wulfe forswolgen'' ("Last night I had [lit. ''dreamed''] a dream that I was devoured by a wolf").

